1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,286
Coach: A shared summary is more than a list of suggestions.

2
00:00:04,286 --> 00:00:08,571
Coach: It says what people support, how far that support goes,

3
00:00:08,571 --> 00:00:10,714
Coach: and what is still unresolved.

4
00:00:10,714 --> 00:00:12,428
Coach: Those are separate claims.

5
00:00:12,428 --> 00:00:18,857
Coach: If one person supports an example, that is not the same as supporting its length,

6
00:00:18,857 --> 00:00:22,714
Coach: its place in the session or the complete plan.

7
00:00:23,064 --> 00:00:25,207
Coach: Return to the taster proposals.

8
00:00:25,207 --> 00:00:29,064
Coach: The following support statements are available in this baseline.

9
00:00:29,064 --> 00:00:32,921
Coach: Read their qualifications as carefully as their positive words.

10
00:00:32,921 --> 00:00:35,921
Coach: A condition is part of the contribution;

11
00:00:35,921 --> 00:00:41,493
Coach: it is not a small detail to delete when you shorten the summary.

12
00:00:41,843 --> 00:00:45,700
Coach: Maya supports showing an example, while protecting pair discussion.

13
00:00:45,700 --> 00:00:47,843
Coach: Noah supports some pair discussion

14
00:00:47,843 --> 00:00:51,700
Coach: and still wants a decision about the example's length.

15
00:00:51,700 --> 00:00:54,271
Coach: These positions have a useful connection,

16
00:00:54,271 --> 00:00:58,557
Coach: but the connection is not yet a fully timed agreement.

17
00:01:06,907 --> 00:01:12,907
Coach: A person who has not replied has not supplied a view you can report.

18
00:01:12,907 --> 00:01:15,050
Coach: Nodding, looking at the board

19
00:01:15,050 --> 00:01:18,907
Coach: or letting a summary pass might have several meanings.

20
00:01:18,907 --> 00:01:20,621
Coach: If the status matters,

21
00:01:20,621 --> 00:01:27,478
Coach: ask a focused question and leave room for a correction or a request for more time.

22
00:01:27,828 --> 00:01:29,542
Coach: You can also overcorrect.

23
00:01:29,542 --> 00:01:34,256
Coach: Saying nothing is agreed can erase a real point of support.

24
00:01:34,256 --> 00:01:37,685
Coach: Keep agreement at the level where it exists:

25
00:01:37,685 --> 00:01:43,685
Coach: a named person's support for a named element, with the words that limit it.

26
00:01:43,685 --> 00:01:48,399
Coach: A complete group decision requires more evidence than a tidy diagram.

27
00:01:48,749 --> 00:01:51,320
Coach: The prompt gives a firm constraint:

28
00:01:51,320 --> 00:01:54,749
Coach: at least five minutes for questions within thirty.

29
00:01:54,749 --> 00:01:59,892
Coach: That is not something you have to infer from a person's reaction.

30
00:01:59,892 --> 00:02:05,892
Coach: Separate the task requirement from the group's preferences so the summary can preserve both

31
00:02:05,892 --> 00:02:09,320
Coach: without calling them the same kind of agreement.

32
00:02:09,670 --> 00:02:13,527
Coach: A useful summary can therefore contain a positive connection

33
00:02:13,527 --> 00:02:15,670
Coach: and an open question together.

34
00:02:15,670 --> 00:02:17,813
Coach: It need not sound indecisive.

35
00:02:17,813 --> 00:02:22,956
Coach: Saying what is supported gives the group a base to work from;

36
00:02:22,956 --> 00:02:28,099
Coach: saying what remains open tells them what their next decision actually concerns.

37
00:02:28,449 --> 00:02:32,306
Coach: Now compare three possible accounts of this same baseline.

38
00:02:32,306 --> 00:02:34,877
Coach: Judge each against the actual statements,

39
00:02:34,877 --> 00:02:38,735
Coach: not just against how confident or friendly it sounds.

40
00:02:38,735 --> 00:02:43,449
Coach: The question is whether the account changes the scope of support

41
00:02:43,449 --> 00:02:45,163
Coach: or discards useful information.

42
00:02:45,513 --> 00:02:50,656
Coach: Choose the account you could defend by pointing back to the source.

43
00:02:50,656 --> 00:02:55,370
Coach: Afterward, notice whether a peer's reply confirms only their own position

44
00:02:55,370 --> 00:02:57,084
Coach: or settles something wider.

45
00:02:57,084 --> 00:03:01,370
Coach: A reply from one person is still one person's reply.

46
00:03:09,720 --> 00:03:13,577
You: There is support for an example and pair discussion,

47
00:03:13,577 --> 00:03:17,863
You: but their timing and the whole sequence are still open.

48
00:03:17,863 --> 00:03:21,291
You: Question time must stay within the thirty minutes.

49
00:03:21,641 --> 00:03:27,212
Noah: Yes, I still want us to decide how short the demonstration should be.

50
00:03:27,562 --> 00:03:32,276
Coach: This summary preserves support for elements while keeping the timing open.

51
00:03:32,276 --> 00:03:35,276
Coach: Noah confirms his own unresolved question here.

52
00:03:35,276 --> 00:03:38,276
Coach: That reply does not certify Maya's view,

53
00:03:38,276 --> 00:03:42,133
Coach: complete the schedule or establish a whole-group decision.

54
00:03:42,483 --> 00:03:46,769
Coach: On any path, return to the scope of the claim.

55
00:03:46,769 --> 00:03:50,197
Coach: The baseline supports keeping useful elements in consideration,

56
00:03:50,197 --> 00:03:54,483
Coach: and it leaves decisions about their arrangement and timing open.

57
00:03:54,483 --> 00:04:00,912
Coach: A summary is ready for use when another person can see what to confirm, qualify

58
00:04:00,912 --> 00:04:01,769
Coach: or correct.

59
00:04:14,119 --> 00:04:17,119
Coach: Use accurate status words when they help:

60
00:04:17,119 --> 00:04:20,548
Coach: proposed, supported with a condition, or still open.

61
00:04:20,548 --> 00:04:24,834
Coach: You do not need to recite labels in every sentence.

62
00:04:24,834 --> 00:04:30,405
Coach: Their job is to stop a provisional idea from becoming a decision simply

63
00:04:30,405 --> 00:04:32,548
Coach: because it has been repeated.

64
00:04:32,898 --> 00:04:35,469
Coach: Once the shared ground is clear,

65
00:04:35,469 --> 00:04:39,327
Coach: the group can test whether the elements fit together.

66
00:04:39,327 --> 00:04:44,041
Coach: That is a different question from whether they all sound attractive.

67
00:04:44,041 --> 00:04:47,041
Coach: The next section looks at the connections

68
00:04:47,041 --> 00:04:50,898
Coach: and constraints that make a combined proposal worth developing.
